WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:05.82
Part five designed the screens and extracted the design system.

00:00:05.82 --> 00:00:08.29
Part six is stage six, the scaffold.

00:00:08.29 --> 00:00:12.06
Hewn generates a working solution for the chosen stack,

00:00:12.06 --> 00:00:16.72
merges it through a pull request once continuous integration passes,

00:00:16.72 --> 00:00:20.76
and then makes those checks required on the default branch.

00:00:21.91 --> 00:00:23.54
Stage six has no agent.

00:00:23.54 --> 00:00:28.56
It reads the stack decision from stage four and picks a stack template.

00:00:28.56 --> 00:00:32.73
The default template is a dot net ten API with xUnit tests,

00:00:32.73 --> 00:00:36.97
an Angular twenty-one workspace with linting and unit tests,

00:00:36.97 --> 00:00:42.77
an empty Playwright suite, and a GitHub Actions workflow with three jobs: backend,

00:00:42.77 --> 00:00:44.46
frontend and end to end.

00:00:44.81 --> 00:00:49.85
The executor has four steps: generate the scaffold, open the pull request,

00:00:49.85 --> 00:00:52.58
wait for checks, then merge and protect.

00:00:53.73 --> 00:00:56.82
The worker still holds no GitHub credential.

00:00:56.82 --> 00:01:01.18
The API gained three mutations: open a pull request, merge it,

00:01:01.18 --> 00:01:06.66
and protect the default branch, plus an endpoint that reads the pull request's

00:01:06.66 --> 00:01:07.16
checks.

00:01:07.16 --> 00:01:12.64
Only stage six may use them, and only for its own branch, hewn slash scaffold.

00:01:12.64 --> 00:01:14.68
Every other stage is refused.

00:01:15.03 --> 00:01:17.49
Every write is keyed by run and step.

00:01:17.49 --> 00:01:23.15
After a resume, the later steps ask to open the pull request again with the same key,

00:01:23.15 --> 00:01:27.15
and get the first one back, so nothing is ever opened twice.

00:01:28.30 --> 00:01:33.45
The check waiter reads the pull request's checks until every template check has

00:01:33.45 --> 00:01:34.10
completed.

00:01:34.10 --> 00:01:38.53
Anything other than success fails the run, naming the failed checks,

00:01:38.53 --> 00:01:39.97
and nothing is merged.

00:01:39.97 --> 00:01:44.27
After the merge, branch protection requires the same three checks,

00:01:44.27 --> 00:01:46.81
so no later change merges without them.

00:01:47.16 --> 00:01:51.21
The template writes files rather than running generators,

00:01:51.21 --> 00:01:53.49
so it is fast and deterministic.

00:01:53.49 --> 00:01:59.10
To prove it really builds, a new CI job in Hewn generates the scaffold on every

00:01:59.10 --> 00:02:02.44
change and runs the scaffold's own CI commands.

00:02:03.59 --> 00:02:07.60
The backend gained eight tests: the branch and path rules,

00:02:07.60 --> 00:02:12.30
opening twice with one key, a failing check, merging and protecting.

00:02:12.30 --> 00:02:17.35
The worker gained six, covering the steps, a failed or unfinished CI run,

00:02:17.35 --> 00:02:19.36
and a stack with no template.

00:02:19.36 --> 00:02:23.93
And the generated scaffold built, linted and passed its own tests,

00:02:23.93 --> 00:02:25.52
both locally and in CI.

00:02:26.67 --> 00:02:30.65
Here is the real app, with one worker and the fake GitHub,

00:02:30.65 --> 00:02:33.33
which runs each workflow job instantly.

00:02:33.33 --> 00:02:34.56
The Owner submits.

00:02:34.56 --> 00:02:39.71
The stages run as before, and stage six commits the scaffold to its branch,

00:02:39.71 --> 00:02:44.52
opens the pull request, sees CI pass, merges, and protects the branch,

00:02:44.52 --> 00:02:46.37
all in a couple of seconds.

00:02:46.37 --> 00:02:48.36
Then the direction completes.

00:02:48.71 --> 00:02:53.34
And this is the scaffold's own Angular app, built from the generated files.

00:02:54.49 --> 00:02:59.46
Stage six now merges a real, tested scaffold through a pull request,

00:02:59.46 --> 00:03:03.56
and the default branch requires its checks from then on.

00:03:03.56 --> 00:03:07.36
Next, stage nine: estimates and automatic descoping.
